00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:02.140
const vs. let vs. var

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.528
These are all ways to 
define variables in Javascript.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.620
const and let were 
introduced in ES6

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.160
but var has been with us
since the beginning.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.660
Let's go over 
their differences.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.740
const is for values 
that never change.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.100
For instance, 
if I have a const Pi

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.900
and I said 
equal to 3.14...

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:24.959
then if I want to 
set Pi again to 1

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.940
Let me run this. 
If you look over on the side

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.551
on the console, you'll see 
uncaught type error assignment

00:00:30.551 --> 00:00:31.859
to constant variable.

00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:34.558
So I can't assign a constant 
to a constant variable.

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:35.940
However if I 
clear my console,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.200
and then I changed this 
const to a var

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.539
and then I run it again

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:41.890
no error

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.689
Just to prove that was running. 
We'll add a console.log

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.419
And you should see a 1 
show up in the console.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:55.200
Now let's go over let. 
let is for block level variables

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.750
So for example,
If I have a for loop

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:05.620
[typing]

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.360
and then for loops 
are always in blocks.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.819
Blocks are anything
 with these curly braces.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.460
let variables, you can see,
I assigned a let variable in the for loop

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.330
and I am going to
 console.log that...

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.450
variable that's 
defined with let

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.540
and as you see 
if I run this

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:30.869
It's gonna say 0, 1, 2. 
So it's locking that variable

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:32.220
I assigned with the let.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.780
Now if I try to console.log 
that variable

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.669
before the for loop 
and then we try running this...

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.250
You can see there's 
going to be an error.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.660
Same if I take this. 
If I copy this...

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.799
and then put it 
after the for loop

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.940
And we would run 
this again.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:49.900
Let me clear the console.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.000
I'm going to run this again

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.519
and you'll see that 
there's another error.

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:56.360
When you define something 
with let, it's only available

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.019
within the block 
that's defined.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.640
We are now going to 
go over to var

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.710
and you'll see 
how it's different.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.140
var is for variables 
available to the entire function

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.069
or if it's not within a function 
to the entire program.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.920
I'm just going to copy 
this whole thing

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.509
and paste it down here.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.860
I am doing this 
with a CodePen,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.360
but I set the keys to be 
similar to Sublime Text.

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:21.700
So if I just 
double click this i

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.520
and I press Ctrl+D 
and so I'll select each i

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.240
and then I'm going 
to press [the key] j

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.240
to switch them 
all to j

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.660
Now this let,
I'm going to switch to var

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.940
because we're going to 
define the variable j

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.200
with var instead of let.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.540
Now what I'm going to do 
is I'm going to

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.320
comment out this 
console.log and let.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.380
We're going to 
run this.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.360
As you can see,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.220
even though this console.log 
is below the for loop,

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.260
it's still able to log it.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.320
Now if I take this 
console.log

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.600
and put one before
the for loop

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.250
the other one is 
after the for loop

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.720
so if I put this one 
before the for loop

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.220
and run this again, 
you'll see in the console

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.420
undefined

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.660
Remember before 
if I assign with let, it said error.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.280
Now It's showing 
undefined

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.180
that means it knows 
the variable exists

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.220
because when you define 
something with var...

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.470
it will be available even 
before the time that you assigned it.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:19.940
It knows it exist, 
but it hasn't been assigned yet,

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.279
so it's showing undefined 
because j does exist,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.200
but it just hasn't been defined 
to any number yet.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.989
So those are basically 
the differences between

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.500
const, var, and let. 
